# Task 01: Static Website on Amazon S3

## The Goal of the Task
To create a static website in S3, deployed via a GitHub/GitLab pipeline.

## Task Resources
Region-specific resources must be created in the ${aws_region} region. For more details about regional services, see AWS Services by Region.

In this task, you will work with the following resources:

S3 Bucket ${bucket_name}: An S3 bucket to host a static website.
IAM User: This IAM user will be used by Github/Gitlab pipelines.
Github/Gitlab Repository: A repository for your website development.
Github/Gitlab Access Token: An Access Token with read/write permissions to your repository.
Github/Gitlab Pipeline: A pipeline for deploying your website to S3.
## Objectives
You must complete the following steps:

## Create an AWS S3 bucket ${bucket_name}, and configure it to host a static website with public access.

## Create an IAM user and grant the necessary privileges.

## Create a Github/Gitlab repository and push an index.html file to it.

## Create a Github/Gitlab Access Token with read/write permissions to your repository.

## Set up a Github/Gitlab pipeline that will deploy your website to S3.
## Verification
To verify that you have successfully completed the task:

Ensure all resources are created with the correct configuration.
Point your web browser to the S3 bucket's website endpoint to check whether the website is deployed and accessible.
## Important Note
Allow up to 3 minutes for your pipeline to complete and S3 synchronization.
Attention! If you use GitHub to complete the task, don’t forget to delete the access_token that was created on GitHub for this task after you finish.

## Deployment Time
It takes up to 2 minutes to deploy task resources.



